    <monitor x="75" precision="0" y="330" height="60" fontSize="15" width="100" display="holding-time">holding-time</monitor>
    <monitor x="210" precision="0" y="415" height="60" fontSize="15" width="100" display="dogs-distance">dogs-distance</monitor>
    <monitor x="75" precision="0" y="415" height="60" fontSize="15" width="100" display="lost-sheep">lost-sheep</monitor>
    <chooser x="75" y="505" height="60" variable="R-repulsion" current="0" width="250" display="R-repulsion">
      <choice type="double" value="3.0"></choice>
      <choice type="double" value="2.5"></choice>
    </chooser>
    <monitor x="75" precision="1" y="600" height="60" fontSize="11" width="115" display="curr. spreadness">flock-spreadness</monitor>
    <monitor x="215" precision="1" y="600" height="60" fontSize="11" width="100" display="mean spreadness">mean-spreadness</monitor>
  </widgets>
  <info><![CDATA[# Sheep Herding Simulation

An agent-based simulation of shepherd dogs herding a flock of sheep. The model studies how the number of dogs needed to complete the herding task scales with flock size, and whether sheep can maintain global flock cohesion from purely local information.

Sheep follow a two-mode force model (grazing vs. fleeing, with fear contagion) and interact with their 7 topological neighbours. While fleeing, their global-cohesion force is computed either from the true global centre of mass, or from a **local density gradient**: each sheep scans eight 45-degree sectors of its neighbourhood and moves toward the densest one. Dogs collect the flock into a circular containment zone, hold it there for 800 ticks, then drive it out through a gate in the right wall.

## Running the model

Open this file with NetLogo (7.0.3 or later). Choose the parameters, click **Setup**, then **Go**. The run succeeds when every sheep has exited through the gate.

## Controls and parameters

- **`nb-sheep`** — Chooser for the flock size (5 to 1000). The scaling analysis used 5, 10, 25, 50, 100, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350 and 400.
- **`nb-dogs`** — Slider for the number of dogs (0 to 35). The scaling analysis used 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 10, 15, 20, 25 and 35.
- **`use-density-gradient?`**
    - **ON:** fleeing sheep head toward their locally densest sector (local information only).
    - **OFF:** fleeing sheep head toward the true global centre of mass (global information).
- **`R-repulsion`** — Chooser for the inter-sheep repulsion radius (3.0 baseline, 2.5 for the sensitivity analysis).

All other flocking and shepherding parameters are fixed as reporter procedures in the Code tab (e.g. `n-topological` = 7, `vision-range` = 30, `W-global-cohesion` = 0.75).

## The three phases

1. **Collecting** — Dogs gather the flock into the containment zone, claiming stray sheep individually and approaching them on a curve.
2. **Holding** — Dogs patrol the zone boundary in assigned sectors for 800 ticks, keeping the flock contained.
3. **Exiting** — Dogs drive the flock toward the gate; every sheep that crosses it is captured.

## Monitors

- **`current-phase`** — Current phase of the run.
- **`sheep-in-zone`** — Sheep currently inside the containment zone.
- **`lost-sheep`** — Sheep too far from the flock centroid.
- **`holding-time`** — Ticks the flock has been held so far.
- **`sheep-captured-count`** — Sheep that have exited through the gate.
- **`dogs-distance`** — Total distance travelled by all dogs.
- **`flock-spreadness`** (shown as "curr. spreadness") — Variance of sheep distances to the flock centroid (lower = more compact).
- **`mean-spreadness`** (shown as "mean spreadness") — Time-averaged flock-spreadness since setup.

## Criterion for completion

The scenario is complete when **all sheep have exited through the gate**. The BehaviorSpace experiments stop each run at success or at 10,000 ticks.

## BehaviorSpace experiments

The experiments in *Tools > BehaviorSpace* reproduce the simulation campaign: the main sweep covers 10 dog counts x 11 flock sizes at 100 runs per condition (11,000 runs), and four `*_R2.5` experiments rerun selected conditions with `R-repulsion` = 2.5.
]]></info>
